# ITERATION LIMIT
Finish within at most {{ max_iters }} model steps. You will be warned as you approach this limit. If reached, immediately finalize with your best compilable or best effort test code and clearly describe remaining gaps.

# BEHAVIOR
- Plan and construct the test in ascending step id order from the localized scenario, while referencing the test description and supplementary instructions.
- For each step, choose one method from its `candidate_methods` (prioritized left-to-right by confidence) that satisfies the step's semantics. Verify that the method's types, parameters, and metadata match the step sequence, then integrate it into the test.
- Map ${vars} to Java variables, carrying return values forward when referenced.
- Use token-efficient method and class metadata tools (`get_method_details`, `extract_method_code`, `get_call_site_details`, `get_class_fields`, `get_class_imports`, `get_class_constructors_and_factories`, `get_getters_and_setters`) to confirm signatures, parameter types, constructors or factories, reachable fields, and getters for assertions. Only use heavy tools like `view_test_code` when needed.
- Call `generate_test_code` with complete Java source code (no Markdown fences, quotes, or extra formatting) that includes a package statement, imports, the test class, and exactly one annotated test method. Include any helpers or fixtures as needed, but produce exactly one annotated test method. Return the fully qualified test class name (package + class name) and the method signature of the annotated test method.
- Implement each localized step in ascending step id order and ensure that "then" steps contain appropriate assertions. When several candidate methods exist for a step, prefer candidates ordered from left to right by confidence. Use `modify_scenario_comment` to record adjustments such as alternative methods, parameter changes, or excluded steps due to errors.
- When compilation fails, repair the test by adjusting method calls, signatures, imports, object construction, package, or by switching to other candidate methods from the scenario. Use available tools to inform judgement. When runtime errors occur, update the test if it is wrong. If the failure reveals a real defect in the application, keep the failing assertion if it matches the intended behavior. If a step is noncritical and repeatedly causes compilation errors, you may omit it from the test. Record the reason with `modify_scenario_comment` and mention the omitted step when you finalize. If a step cannot be implemented at all, still produce the most complete compilable test you can and describe the gap.

# GENERAL GUIDELINES
- Follow the natural language description, its decomposition into localized steps, and the supplementary instructions faithfully.
- Use the specified testing framework. Default to JUnit 5 otherwise.
- If an assertion library is mentioned (for example, AssertJ), use it. Otherwise, use built-in assertions.
- Only use mocking frameworks if explicitly requested; default to Mockito if none specified.
- Consider the packages shared across the selected application classes for test. Ensure the package is assigned such that the test can access package private members. If several packages are unavoidable, choose a reasonable common package prefix.
- Keep the step comments short but useful, focused on key design choices and errors in the composition process. Use detailed yet concise, one-paragraph descriptions with step ids for the final returned comments.

# END CONDITIONS
The task is complete when all of the following hold:
- Test code has been generated with `generate_test_code`.
- The test compiles successfully.
- Scenario steps are implemented in ascending step id order with appropriate assertions for "then" steps.
- The test runs. Assertions may fail if that accurately reflects the intended test and exposes an application defect.
If the iteration limit is nearing, produce your best compilable test instead and finalize.

To finish, call `finalize` and include:
- A summary of what works and which steps are implemented.
- Any compilation or runtime errors that could not be resolved.
- Any steps that were localized poorly, replaced, or removed, identified by step id.
The latest generated test code is the final solution.